fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1103", description: some(value: "A DERIVED provision is REFUSED BY THE NEXT PHASE's provider-coverage check, so a KB that loaded clean cannot be loaded into again -- `load_stdlib` then `load_incremental` fails on WI-664's own derived `NonEq` rows.\n\nMEASURED, at 51d17d22 (i.e. BEFORE WI-1098 -- this is WI-664's, not WI-1098's; the same\nprobe fails identically with WI-1098's whole source change stashed):\n\n  let mut kb = KnowledgeBase::new();\n  load::load_stdlib(&mut kb, &stdlib_and_rust_bindings, &NullResolver)   -- OK\n  load::load_incremental(&mut kb, &[&any_user_file], &NullResolver)      -- FAILS:\n\n    'anthill.reflect.Expr' provides 'anthill.prelude.NonEq' but backs no operation\n     'anthill.prelude.NonEq.nonEqRefl' (no default on 'anthill.prelude.NonEq', no own\n     'nonEqRefl' on 'anthill.reflect.Expr')\n    ... and the same for anthill.reflect.TermRepr, anthill.reflect.LiteralRepr,\n        anthill.geometry.EulerAngles, anthill.geometry.Vec3\n\nThose five are EXACTLY the composites `eq_derive` classifies Partial and derives\n`NonEq` for. The user file is irrelevant -- an empty namespace fails the same way.\n\nTHE MECHANISM IS THE PLACEMENT TRICK, AND IT IS SINGLE-PHASE ONLY. `eq_derive::run`\nstands AFTER `check_provider_operations` precisely so a derived `NonEq`'s `nonEqRefl`\nwitness is not held to op-backing (it is a propagated classification, not a declared\nprimitive -- eq_derive.rs's module header states this as the reason for its position).\nWithin ONE phase that works. But the FACT persists in the KB, and every subsequent\n`load_phase_inner` runs `check_provider_operations` again -- now with the derived rows\nalready in the relation, and this time the check reaches them BEFORE eq_derive re-runs.\nSo the dodge protects the row that is being created and not the row that already exists.\n\nWHY THE SUITE IS GREEN: nearly every test builds a KB with ONE `load_all`. The\ntwo-phase path is exercised by `incremental_load_test`, whose fixtures load\n`stdlib_dir()` ALONE -- without `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/`, so `Int64`/`String`\nnever provide `Eq`, so almost nothing classifies at all and the five carriers above are\nnot reached. That is the same \"corpus cannot reach the bug\" shape as WI-979.\n\nTWO DIRECTIONS, and the choice is the work:\n (a) MARK a derived provision, and have the provider-coverage checks skip it. Honest --\n     the checks are about what an AUTHOR claimed -- but `SortProvidesInfo` is\n     deliberately byte-identical across every producer (load.rs `load_provides_conditions`\n     records that four places hard-code its field list), so a third field is not free.\n (b) Make the checks phase-aware -- only check provisions asserted THIS phase. The\n     loader already tracks per-phase `LoadResult.fact_rule_ids`, so the information\n     exists; the question is whether \"checked once, ever\" is the right contract for a\n     check whose premise (a backing operation) a later phase could remove.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - `load_stdlib` + `load_incremental` over the FULL closure (stdlib + anthill-stl,\n   i.e. `common::collect_stdlib_and_rust_bindings`) loads clean, DRIVEN, with a\n   user file that uses a derived-`NonEq` carrier so the classification is not vacuous;\n - `incremental_load_test`'s fixtures move to the full closure, or say at their site\n   why the stdlib-alone corpus is the subject -- as written they cannot reach this;\n - CONTROL: a genuinely unbacked HAND-WRITTEN provision is still refused in phase 2,\n   with the same message it gets in phase 1 (whichever direction is taken must not\n   turn the check off);\n - say at each test site which tests fail when the change is backed out;\n - full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found while driving a code-review finding on WI-1098 (a suspected cross-phase\nEq/NonEq disagreement, which turned out to be unreachable because the load fails first,\nfor this reason). Related: WI-664 (the derivation), WI-1008/WI-1049 (per-phase resets)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-14T20:21:43Z"))
